aves different shapes  the great idea which we can apply to  every fact about living creatures is  that these facts usually have uses 12  why do the leaves of the aspen always  shake the shaking of the leaves has the  same effect as if the trees were Fanning  themselves 13 why does oil make a wheel  go round more easily it all depends on  where the oil is put  14 why do we see the stars only at night  the stars are shining all the time  sending light to earth but more than  this is needed for us to see 15 is an  atom living it is almost a living thing  16 can country people see writ small  better than townspeople if country folk  use their eyes mainly at distances their  vision will be keenest at distances 17  why does a lump rise in my throat when I  cry it is the place of speech which is  the most marvelous thing finally it is a  yale college tradition to read a passage  by john milton on this occasion you know  the story i think there were these two  young 20-somethings maybe even teenagers  in a garden they were in a perfect place  and they knew that they also knew that  there was a world beyond and they  couldn't help but want to know about it  yes as in the Zarin poem there was  always a thirst for knowledge and then  you remember that in the garden there  was this wardrobe malfunction like you  and as you may particularly feel on  Monday afternoon or tuesday morning  their departure seemed to them sudden  unwarranted abrupt inevitable from  Paradise Lost by John Milton 1667 some  natural tears they dropped but wiped  them soon the world was all before them  where to choose their place of rest and  Providence their guide they hand in hand  with wandering steps and slow through  Eden  took their solitary way congratulations  class of 2013  you  good afternoon I want to begin by  welcoming all the parents relatives and  friends of our graduates to you parents  especially I want to say that I know  just how you feel Jane and I have had  the good fortune of watching all four of  our children graduate from college and  we know the pride and the pleasure that  you are experiencing and the many  memories that such an occasion conjures  up and I also know that what you would  really like to do right now is to stand  up and join those of us on stage in  offering warm congratulations to the  yale college class of 2013  and now I would like to ask the class of  2013 in turn to reflect for one moment  on how you came to reach this milestone  during that moment of reflection one of  the things that may have occurred to you  is that there are many people here today  your parents siblings relatives and  friends whose support and encouragement  made your achievements here possible I  bet you'd like to rise and show them  your appreciation  we leave together you leave yale college  after four years i leave the yale  presidency after twenty I find myself  thinking about the words of a grateful  dead song written in 1970 the year I  came to graduate school at Yale it's  been a long trip but for all of us more  wonderful than strange each of you has  had the opportunity to learn from  teachers who are among the world's most  brilliant scholars and to grow in the  company of some of the most exceptional  young adults to be found anywhere your  classmates you've had access to  extraordinary research laboratories  libraries museums and musical and  dramatic performances you've lived in  for years for four years in an  environment of high energy and high  achievement in the classroom and the  newsroom on the stage and the athletic  field in the concert hall and the art  studio in community service and in  political debate in the course of all  this activity you have come to know  yourselves more completely these  pleasures now give way to a new set of  challenges and opportunities as you  explore the world that is all before you  some of you know exactly what you want  to do in the years ahead whether it is  medicine the law the military starting a  company or this year in particular  playing professional hockey  congratulations we wish you every  success as for the rest of us who are  not so sure I feel for myself I feel  myself for the first time in 20 years of  baccalaureate addresses in true  communion with you you and I are going  to need time to figure out what's next  time to consider where our true passions  lie and how we might give them the  fullest expression it's not an easy  world out there we are still suffering  from the effects of the most severe  recession since the Great Depression and  the American political system wants a  global model of effective bipartisan  governance has become so polarized that  it is nearly paralyzed it seems  astonishing but this spring in the  aftermath of a mass murder at an  elementary school just 25 miles from  here the US Congress could not manage to  restore even the most moderate  restrictions on the sale of assault  weapons to private citizens and gun  control is just one of many critical  issues on which this nation appears to  be at impasse how about ensuring an  adequate K through 12 education for all  Americans  how about repairing our congested  highways deteriorating bridges  substandard airports and obsolete  passenger rail service how about  supporting scientific advance and  innovation the innovation that flows  from it how about resetting our  expectations about entitlements and how  about concerted action to mitigate the  severe economic and ecological  consequences of global warming  right now you might be you might not be  thinking first and foremost about these  issues you quite properly may be  thinking instead about where you will  find an apartment whether you will find  a job and if you already have one  whether you will like it you may also be  thinking about whether and when to apply  to graduate or professional school and  more generally about how these near term  choices might help you build a life that  is personally and professionally  fulfilling because Yale has encouraged  you to think this way you may also be  contemplating how the lives you are  starting to create might make a  difference to others to your families  and your communities and how you might  be of service as so many of you have  been as students to causes larger than  your own personal fulfillment I am  thinking about my next steps to and just  like you I'm hoping to find work that is  personally and professionally fulfilling  and that makes a difference in the lives  of others uncertainty about these  matters is a source of anxiety for us  all for you and for me but I think we're  up to it you have overcome a lot of  challenges as you've worked your way  through Yale and so if I there's plenty  of upside ahead at what we economists  call the micro level the immediate  personal and professional spheres that  we occupy but what about the macro level  what about the big picture issues that  lately have been so inadequately  addressed by our political processes  these may not be at the center of your  current plans but let me suggest that  there's plenty of opportunity here too  in my explanation I will focus  principally on the United States but  every country in the world faces similar  questions about education infrastructure  innovation and tie  elements and climate change the answers  to these questions are going to have a  huge impact on your personal and  professional lives affecting your  incomes your security your health and  your well-being your involvement with  these issues can make a difference  consider K through 12 education for more  than a century American primary and  secondary education was the envy of the  world producing the world's most  literate labor force and creating a huge  competitive advantage for the United  States as North America and Europe  industrialized today on the most widely  used international test of comparative  achievement the performance of  fifteen-year-old Americans ranks 14th  and 